1637 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 455 0 R
/E (Deployment is the process of making the Smart Expense Splitter and Group Budget Management System available for use by end users. The deployment process involves setting up the development environment, configuring the database, installing required dependencies, running the backend server, launching the frontend application, and verifying system functionality. Proper deployment ensures that all components of the application work together efficiently and reliably.)
/Pg 75 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
1638 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 455 0 R
/T (The first step in deployment is installing the necessary software and development tools. Visual Studio Code is used as the development environment, Node.js and npm are installed to support backend development, and PostgreSQL is installed as the database management system. On macOS, PostgreSQL can be installed using Homebrew, while Windows and Linux users can install PostgreSQL through the official installer packages. After installation, the PostgreSQL service is started and verified to ensure that database operations can be performed successfully.)
/E (The first step in deployment is installing the necessary software and development tools. Visual Studio Code is used as the development environment, Node.js and npm are installed to support backend development, and PostgreSQL is installed as the database management system. On macOS, PostgreSQL can be installed using Homebrew, while Windows and Linux users can install PostgreSQL through the official installer packages. After installation, the PostgreSQL service is started and verified to ensure that database operations can be performed successfully.)
/Pg 75 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
1639 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 455 0 R
/T (Once the required software is installed, the project source code is obtained from the GitHub repository or copied into the local development machine. The project directory is opened in Visual Studio Code, and all frontend and backend files are verified. The backend dependencies specified in the package.json file are then installed using the npm install command. This process downloads all required libraries, including Express.js, pg \(node-postgres\), dotenv, and other supporting packages needed by the application.)
/E (Once the required software is installed, the project source code is obtained from the GitHub repository or copied into the local development machine. The project directory is opened in Visual Studio Code, and all frontend and backend files are verified. The backend dependencies specified in the package.json file are then installed using the npm install command. This process downloads all required libraries, including Express.js, pg \(node-postgres\), dotenv, and other supporting packages needed by the application.)
/Pg 75 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
1640 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 455 0 R
/T (The next step involves configuring the PostgreSQL database. A new database is created using PostgreSQL administration tools or SQL commands. The schema.sql file is executed to create the required tables, including expense_groups, members, expenses, and expense_splits. Primary keys, foreign keys, constraints, and relationships are established during this process. After database creation, connection details such as database name, username, password, host, and port number are stored in a .env file to ensure secure configuration management.)
/E (The next step involves configuring the PostgreSQL database. A new database is created using PostgreSQL administration tools or SQL commands. The schema.sql file is executed to create the required tables, including expense_groups, members, expenses, and expense_splits. Primary keys, foreign keys, constraints, and relationships are established during this process. After database creation, connection details such as database name, username, password, host, and port number are stored in a .env file to ensure secure configuration management.)
/Pg 75 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
1641 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 455 0 R
/T (After database configuration, the backend server is prepared for execution. The application reads environment variables from the .env file using the dotenv package and establishes a connection with PostgreSQL through the pg driver. The server is then started using npm start or node server.js. Once execution begins successfully, the backend becomes available through localhost and starts listening for incoming API requests. The server logs are monitored to verify successful startup and database connectivity.)
/E (After database configuration, the backend server is prepared for execution. The application reads environment variables from the .env file using the dotenv package and establishes a connection with PostgreSQL through the pg driver. The server is then started using npm start or node server.js. Once execution begins successfully, the backend becomes available through localhost and starts listening for incoming API requests. The server logs are monitored to verify successful startup and database connectivity.)
/Pg 75 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
1642 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 455 0 R
